    },
    {
      "area": "Control and application upgrade transactions",
      "inspection": "Freshly traced trusted separate-source control update, barriers and twice-confirmed stopped process/cgroup state, immutable generation/launcher recovery and terminal publication outside compensation. Control update intentionally leaves sudo unchanged; config-layer migration transaction installs current sudo even when system-v1 registry already exists. Default Manager.verify remains strict current-unit/current-sudo. Only restore-services supplies its active lifecycle journal path for exact supported worker/sudo restoration. The helper independently requires private root-owned record, marker digest, matching restoring journal, unique transaction, original config/control/identity/release binding, full ownership and saved exact bytes/mode. Other account, state, release, provider and HTTPS checks remain. Legacy results explicitly label retained capability and preserve original activity; failed verification re-blocks and stops services without clearing pending recovery."
    },
    {
      "area": "Archives, ownership, request spool and failure classification",
      "inspection": "Reviewed archive/manifest and dependency input bounds, stable descriptors, root/private ownership and service identities, source/import durability, request and receipt budgets, no unsafe retry after dispatch, and distinction between operator CLI errors and RPC not_sent/unknown contracts. Fresh stopped-journal review confirms pending becomes not_sent and dispatching becomes unknown only after both service cgroups stop; historical and retired-operation evidence is retained."
    },
